## ADS1115 ALERT Safety Interface

The ADS1115 ALERT output shall not directly energize any relay coil.

A single-channel 5V opto-isolated relay module with onboard transistor
driver and flyback protection shall be used as the interface between
the ADS1115 ALERT output and the contactor authorization circuit.

This relay module replaces the previously proposed automotive relay,
eliminating unnecessary hardware while protecting the ADS1115 from
relay-coil current.


=========================================================
STATE WITNESSES
=========================================================

The controller should compare several independent observations
before concluding that the pack is operating in the expected state.

WITNESS 1 — SOFTWARE COMMAND

• What the ESP32 requested.

Examples:
  - 12V commanded
  - 16V commanded
  - All contactors commanded OFF

---------------------------------------------------------

WITNESS 2 — CONTACTOR AUXILIARY CONTACTS

• LEV100 auxiliary contacts indicate mechanical contactor state.

• Auxiliary contacts support the decision but do not by
  themselves prove the actual output voltage.

---------------------------------------------------------

WITNESS 3 — FINAL OUTPUT VOLTAGE

• Measure the final positive and final negative output
  immediately before the jump cables leave the enclosure.

• Software calculates the actual cable-to-cable output voltage.

• This provides the strongest evidence of whether the pack is
  actually producing 12V, 16V, or neither.

---------------------------------------------------------

WITNESS 4 — CURRENT MEASUREMENT

• OTC current sensor indicates both current magnitude and
  direction.

• Used to confirm:

  - Load/cranking event started
  - Heavy-current event ended
  - Reverse charging current detected
  - Cold-charge protection events
  - Pack resistance calculations

=========================================================
EXPECTED AGREEMENT
=========================================================

12V EXPECTED

Command:
  12V ON
  16V OFF

Auxiliary:
  LEV100 engaged
  EV200 released

Output:
  Approximately 4-cell output voltage


16V EXPECTED

Command:
  12V OFF
  16V ON

Auxiliary:
  LEV100 released
  EV200 engaged

Output:
  Approximately 5-cell output voltage

=========================================================
DISAGREEMENT EXAMPLES
=========================================================

Example:

Command:
  16V

Auxiliary:
  EV200 engaged

Output:
  12V

Possible causes:

• Failed contactor
• Loose busbar
• Wiring fault
• Failed output path
• Incorrect voltage measurement


Example:

Command:
  16V

Auxiliary:
  Open

Output:
  16V

Possible causes:

• Failed auxiliary contact
• Auxiliary wiring fault
• Welded contactor
• Mechanical mismatch


Example:

Command:
  12V

Auxiliary:
  Both contactors indicate engaged

Possible action:

• Immediate processor lockout
• Remove positive authorization
• Log critical interlock fault
• Display fault information

=========================================================
CASE-MOUNTED BOOST BUTTON
=========================================================

The case-mounted BOOST button shall request 16V operation
through the normal break-before-make sequence.

Press:
  Request 16V.

Release:
  Request return to 12V.

The BOOST button shall never bypass hardware interlocks or
break-before-make timing.
